  "notes": "The real v3 project response groups versions by minor line (\"1.21\": [...], \"26.2\": [...], ...); listVersions() flattens every group into one list, then sorts descending with compareMCVersions (ServerJarProviders.swift:123), which splits on \".\" and drops any component that isn't a bare integer -- it does NOT try to parse a trailing \"-rc-2\"/\"-pre7\" suffix as part of the number. That drop, not a special pre-release rule, is what produces two real surprises visible in this exact response: \"26.2-rc-2\" sorts as if its version were just \"26\" (its second component \"2-rc-2\" fails Int parsing and is dropped, so it's compared as [26] vs [26,1,1] and loses on the second slot), landing below the three real 26.x releases instead of near them; and \"1.13-pre7\" sorts as bare \"1\" for the same reason, landing dead last -- below even 1.7.10 -- despite looking like a 1.13 version to a human reader. Preserve this exact drop-non-numeric-component behavior in the Rust port; do not \"fix\" it by trying to parse pre-release suffixes, since nothing downstream depends on pre-release versions sorting sensibly and MSC 1 never attempted to.",
